# transtation server configuration.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THIS FILE. Copy it to .env and edit. Every value here is
# optional — with no .env at all the server provisions itself and works.
#
# These are CONFIG, not identity. They are read fresh on every boot, so
# changing one and running `docker compose up -d` takes effect immediately.
# Your Reality keypair and user UUIDs live in the data volume and are never
# touched by anything in this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- networking

# Public port clients connect to. 443 blends in best; change it only if
# something else on the host already owns 443.
#PORT=443

# Public address clients dial. Auto-detected from cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace
# on first boot. Set it if the host is behind NAT, has several public IPs, or
# you point a domain at it.
#HOST=203.0.113.10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- camouflage

# The real site your server impersonates.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET. On first boot transtation picks one, the way a commercial
# VPN picks your nearest exit rather than handing you a server list.
#
# What it optimises for, in order:
#
#  1. Can the name carry your traffic without looking wrong? Your clients' ISP
#     sees their traffic labelled with this name and judges the VOLUME AND
#     SHAPE of it against what the name implies. Hours of sustained multi-
#     gigabyte download addressed to a corporate brochure page is not a
#     plausible thing for a human to be doing, and DPI drops it. The same bytes
#     pulled from a video service are what everyone does all evening. So
#     candidates are classed bulk / mixed / light, and this dominates the score.
#  2. Does REALITY actually work with it? TLS 1.3 and a certificate chain with
#     headroom under the size where the handshake silently dies.
#  3. How far away is it? Measured TLS handshake time, because your server
#     re-dials this site on every client connection.
#  4. Is it in the same region as the traffic? Worth 150ms of latency.
#
# The choice is STICKY. It is stored in the data volume and never re-picked on
# its own, because every share link carries `sni=` and silently changing it
# would break every client you have already configured.
#
#   transtation sni              what was chosen, and why
#   transtation sni auto         probe again and re-pick   (invalidates links)
#   transtation sni <domain>     set by hand               (invalidates links)
#
# Set this only to override permanently, and follow it with `transtation
# verify`. Two hard requirements: TLS 1.3, and a chain under about 5200 bytes.
# `www.microsoft.com` (5879) fails the second one silently -- healthy server,
# and every client gets the real website instead. Apple and iCloud domains are
# rejected outright: Xray warns they get your IP blocked by the GFW.
#SNI=dl.google.com

# The address the Reality handshake is actually forwarded to. Defaults to
# $SNI:443 and you almost never need to set this separately.
#DEST=dl.google.com:443

# Where your clients connect FROM, as a two-letter country code. The DPI that
# judges your traffic belongs to their ISP, not to your server's datacentre, so
# this is the region the dest should look native to. Defaults to the server's
# own country, which is right when your server is near or relevant to your
# users. Set it when it is not -- clients in Korea using a server in Brazil,
# say.
#CLIENT_REGION=KR

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- egress

# Cloudflare WARP egress. On by default: your traffic leaves from a Cloudflare
# IP instead of your VPS's, so the destination never sees your server's address
# and cloud-IP blocklists do not apply.
#
# Set WARP=0 to egress directly from the VPS IP instead. That is simpler and
# faster, but exposes the address that is tied to your name and payment method.
# It also means outbound port 25 is not blocked for you by Cloudflare — which
# is why transtation blocks it itself.
#WARP=1

# WARP endpoint. Pinned to a literal IP on purpose: resolving
# engage.cloudflareclient.com can return an IPv6 address on a host with no IPv6
# route, and Xray picks between the A and AAAA records at random. Override only
# if this anycast address does not handshake from your provider.
#WARP_ENDPOINT=162.159.192.1:2408

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- clients

# Name of the user created on first boot.
#USER_NAME=main

# Minimum client version the Reality server accepts. transtation pins this to
# 0.0.0 so no client is ever silently rejected. Newer Xray versions default it
# to a real floor, and the failure mode is nasty: the server starts fine, the
# port listens, and rejected clients see the real target website instead of an
# error. Raise it only if you know every client you own is newer.
#MIN_CLIENT_VER=0.0.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ advanced

# Skip the startup TLS probe against $SNI. The probe only warns; it never
# blocks startup. Set to 1 on hosts with slow or filtered outbound HTTPS.
#SKIP_DEST_CHECK=0
